= Quantum information
{scope}
{title2=A16608W1}

Solutions to all three questions of the 11 June 2020 paper. https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/ba_computer_science_past_papers/response/2577745/attach/2/202402%20183%20CS%20Papers.zip?cookie_passthrough=1[Released Oxford examination archive], member `2019/T/a16608w1.pdf`. The dated cover establishes 2020 despite the archive directory. All displayed state <vectors> are normalized unless described as unnormalized conditional <vectors>. We use column <vectors>, $|ij\rangle=|i\rangle\otimes|j\rangle$, and the left tensor factor as the first wire. In a product of gates, the rightmost gate acts first. A <global phase> is physically irrelevant, but all relative signs and phases below are retained.

== 1
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

For the three given <qutrit> <vectors>, $\langle\phi_0|=(\langle0|-i\langle2|)/\sqrt2$ and $\langle\phi_2|=(\langle0|+i\langle2|)/\sqrt2$. Taking the <complex conjugate> in the bra is essential. Their squared <norms> are $1,1,1$, and
$$
\langle\phi_0|\phi_2\rangle=\tfrac12\{1+(-i)(-i)\}=0,\qquad \langle\phi_0|\phi_1\rangle=\langle\phi_2|\phi_1\rangle=0.
$$
Thus they are an <orthonormal set> of three <vectors> in the three-dimensional <inner product space> $\mathbb C^3$. Such a set is <linearly independent> and spans the space, so **it is an <orthonormal basis>**.

=== b
{scope}

For an <orthonormal basis>, choosing coefficients with magnitudes $\sqrt{p_j}$ gives the required <Born rule> <probabilities>. More generally, $\sum_j\sqrt{p_j}e^{i\theta_j}|\phi_j\rangle$ realizes the same distribution for arbitrary phases. The following are convenient examples of <pure-state realization of a basis measurement distribution>.

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Choose **$|\psi\rangle=|0\rangle$**. Since $|0\rangle=(|\phi_0\rangle+|\phi_2\rangle)/\sqrt2$, the <probability amplitudes> in the measurement <basis> are $(1/\sqrt2,0,1/\sqrt2)$. Their squared moduli give $(1/2,0,1/2)$ by the <Born rule>.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Choose **$|\psi\rangle=|\phi_2\rangle$**. Its <inner products> with the three measurement <vectors> are $(0,0,1)$, so the <Born rule> gives certainty for the outcome labelled 2 and zero <probability> for the other two outcomes. This uses the measurement <basis>, rather than assuming that its label 2 means the initial state must be the computational <vector> $|2\rangle$.

==== iii
{scope}

===== Solution

Choose
$$
\boxed{|\psi\rangle=\frac{|\phi_0\rangle+|\phi_1\rangle}{\sqrt2}=\frac12|0\rangle+\frac1{\sqrt2}|1\rangle+\frac i2|2\rangle.}
$$
<Orthonormality> makes this a <unit vector>. Its <probability amplitudes> in the given measurement <basis> are $(1/\sqrt2,1/\sqrt2,0)$, so the required <probabilities> are $(1/2,1/2,0)$.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Swap the first two computational <vectors> and fix the third:
$$
\boxed{U=|1\rangle\langle0|+|0\rangle\langle1|+|2\rangle\langle2|=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\1&0&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
This <permutation matrix> has <orthonormal> columns; equivalently, $U^\dagger=U$ and $U^2=I$, proving <unitarity>. Its first column gives $U|0\rangle=|1\rangle=|\phi_1\rangle$, as required. A unitary must also specify what happens to the rest of the <basis>, which is why a single outer product alone would not suffice.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

The map sends $|0\rangle$ to $|0\rangle$, $|1\rangle$ to $|2\rangle$, and $|2\rangle$ to $\lambda|1\rangle$. These output columns are mutually <orthogonal>, with squared <norms> $1,1,|\lambda|^2$. Consequently
$$
M_\lambda^\dagger M_\lambda=\operatorname{diag}(1,1,|\lambda|^2),
$$
and
$$
\boxed{M_\lambda\text{ is unitary if and only if }|\lambda|=1,\text{ equivalently }\lambda=e^{i\theta}\text{ for real }\theta.}
$$
Necessity follows from preserving the <norm> of $|2\rangle$; sufficiency follows because the three columns are then an <orthonormal basis>. Merely requiring $\lambda\ne0$ would establish invertibility but not <unitarity>. This is the <weighted permutation unitarity criterion>.

=== e
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Directly applying the <Hadamard gate> gives
$$
H|+i\rangle=\frac12\{(1+i)|0\rangle+(1-i)|1\rangle\}=e^{i\pi/4}|-i\rangle,
$$
while
$$
H|-i\rangle=\frac12\{(1-i)|0\rangle+(1+i)|1\rangle\}=e^{-i\pi/4}|+i\rangle.
$$
Hence **the two states are interchanged up to the displayed <global phases>**. Their relative factors $-i$ and $+i$ are retained. They are the two <Pauli eigenstates> of the <Pauli Y gate>, and the <Hadamard interchange of Y eigenstates> is consistent with $HYH=-Y$.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Compute the parity into the second wire, phase that wire with the <T gate>, then uncompute with the same <controlled-NOT gate>. The answer is
$$
\boxed{G=\mathrm{CNOT}\,(I\otimes T)\,\mathrm{CNOT}.}
$$
For computational bits $x,y$, the successive states are $|x,y\rangle\mapsto|x,x\oplus y\rangle\mapsto e^{i\pi(x\oplus y)/4}|x,x\oplus y\rangle\mapsto e^{i\pi(x\oplus y)/4}|x,y\rangle$. The parity $x\oplus y$ is one precisely on the two odd-parity inputs. Thus, in the ordered <computational basis> $00,01,10,11$, the <matrix> is exactly $\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\pi/4},e^{i\pi/4},1)$, with no omitted overall phase. This is a <two-qubit parity phase gate>.

==== iii
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $R=\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\pi/12})$. Since the <Hadamard gate> maps the standard <basis> to $|+\rangle,|-\rangle$, the given gate is $Q=HRH$. Because $H^2=I$,
$$
Q^3=HR^3H=HTH,\qquad\boxed{T=HQ^3H.}
$$
Next, use $HZH=X$ on the target wire of the <controlled-Z gate>:
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{CNOT}=(I\otimes H)\,CZ\,(I\otimes H).}
$$
The control-zero block remains the identity, and the control-one block becomes $HZH=X$, proving the identity with the stated control convention. We have therefore constructed each gate in the assumed <universal quantum gate set> $\{\mathrm{CNOT},H,T\}$ using only $CZ,Q,H$.

Replacing every gate of a circuit by its displayed finite construction reproduces that circuit exactly. Thus **$\{CZ,Q,H\}$ is universal** under the usual arbitrary-accuracy convention: it can perform every approximation obtainable from the given universal set. The claim concerns approximation of arbitrary <unitary operators>; it does not assert that finitely many circuits exactly represent an uncountable continuum of gates. This is <universality transfer by exact gate synthesis>.

=== f
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

The <controlled-NOT gate> is its own inverse, so $\mathrm{CNOT}^2=I$. Every remaining nontrivial gate acts on one wire. By the <tensor product> rule, any circuit on a product input stays a <product state>; in particular, starting at $|00\rangle$ it cannot produce a <Bell state>.

This also prevents arbitrary approximation, not merely exact preparation. For normalized single-qubit <vectors> $|u\rangle,|v\rangle$,
$$
|\langle\Phi^+|u\otimes v\rangle|^2=\tfrac12|u_0v_0+u_1v_1|^2\leq\tfrac12
$$
by the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality>. Hence the <quantum fidelity> with $|\Phi^+\rangle$ is bounded away from one for every reachable state. **This gate set is not universal.** The obstruction is preservation of <product states>, a special case of the existing <product-state obstruction to SWAP universality>.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Here $H^2=I$. Both remaining gates fix the all-zero computational <vector>: the <T gate> fixes $|0\rangle$, and the <controlled-NOT gate> fixes $|00\rangle$. Therefore every finite circuit, on any number of initially zero wires, leaves $|0\cdots0\rangle$ unchanged.

It cannot even approximate $|+\rangle\otimes|0\cdots0\rangle$, whose squared overlap with that unchanged <vector> is $1/2$. Thus **the gate set is not universal**. The needed <quantum superposition> cannot be created from this input by <basis> permutations and diagonal phases alone.

==== iii
{scope}

===== Solution

Now $T^2=S=\operatorname{diag}(1,i)$. The gates $H,S$ and CNOT are <Clifford gates>: conjugating a <Pauli matrix> or a <tensor product> of them gives another signed Pauli tensor. On one wire,
$$
HXH=Z,\quad HZH=X,\quad SXS^\dagger=Y,\quad SZS^\dagger=Z.
$$
For CNOT the generator images are $X\otimes I\mapsto X\otimes X$, $I\otimes X\mapsto I\otimes X$, $Z\otimes I\mapsto Z\otimes I$, and $I\otimes Z\mapsto Z\otimes Z$. These identities can be checked on the four computational <vectors> and generate the preservation property for all Pauli tensors.<The Heisenberg Representation of Quantum Computers by Daniel Gottesman>[Section 3 and Table 1, Clifford generators and their Pauli conjugation rules]{ref}

For a fixed number $n$ of wires there are only finitely many possible images of the $2n$ generators $X_j,Z_j$ in the <Pauli group>. Their images determine a unitary up to <global phase>. To see this, if two <unitary operators> induce identical images, their quotient commutes with every $Z_j$, so is diagonal in the <computational basis>; commuting with every $X_j$ then forces all diagonal entries to be equal. The quotient is a scalar phase. Thus the generated gates form a finite set modulo <global phase>, which cannot be dense in the continuous space of all <unitary operators>. This is <finiteness of the projective Clifford group>.

A small visible consequence is that from $|0\rangle$, one-qubit <Clifford circuits> reach only the six states $|0\rangle,|1\rangle,|+\rangle,|-\rangle,|+i\rangle,|-i\rangle$ up to <global phase>. For instance, $T|+\rangle$ is outside this finite set. The fixed-$n$ Pauli argument also covers circuits using CNOT on several wires. Therefore **$\{\mathrm{CNOT},H,T^2\}$ is not universal**.

== 2
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Write the <product state> as $|\Psi\rangle=|a\rangle\otimes|b\rangle$. The two <partial inner products> are
$$
|\psi'_j\rangle=(\langle\psi_j|a\rangle)|b\rangle=c_j|b\rangle,\qquad j=1,2.
$$
Both <vectors> therefore lie in the same one-dimensional <vector subspace>, possibly with one or both equal to zero. If $(c_1,c_2)\ne(0,0)$, choose $\lambda_1=c_2$ and $\lambda_2=c_1$; then $\lambda_1|\psi'_1\rangle=\lambda_2|\psi'_2\rangle$, and the multipliers are not both zero. If both $c_j$ vanish, choose $(\lambda_1,\lambda_2)=(1,0)$.

Thus **the conditional <vectors> are <linearly dependent>**, with a nontrivial pair of multipliers in every case. The printed existential condition does not explicitly exclude $(0,0)$, which would make it vacuous for arbitrary <vectors>; the argument establishes the stronger intended statement. These unnormalized <vectors> need not themselves be valid unit state <vectors>, and a zero <vector> corresponds to a measurement outcome of <probability> zero. This is the forward direction of the <conditional-vector product-state criterion>.

=== b
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Factor the coefficients:
$$
|\Psi_1\rangle=\left(\frac{|0\rangle-|1\rangle}{\sqrt2}\right)\otimes\left(\frac{|0\rangle-|1\rangle}{\sqrt2}\right)=|-\rangle\otimes|-\rangle.
$$
Therefore **the first state is a <product state>, not <entangled>**. Both factors have unit <norm>, so this factorization also checks <normalization>.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Taking <partial inner products> with $\langle0|$ and $\langle1|$ on the first wire gives $(|0\rangle+|1\rangle)/2$ and $(-|0\rangle+|1\rangle)/2$. They are nonzero and <orthogonal>, hence <linearly independent>. This contradicts the necessary product-state property proved in part (a), so **the second state is <entangled>**.

Equivalently, its <tensor-product coefficient matrix> is $C=\tfrac12\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}1&1\\-1&1\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$, with $\det C=1/2\ne0$. The <two-qubit product-state determinant criterion> gives the same conclusion. In fact $CC^\dagger=I/2$, so its <Schmidt coefficients> are both $1/\sqrt2$: it is maximally <entangled>.

==== iii
{scope}

===== Solution

The corresponding conditional <vectors> are $|0\rangle/\sqrt2$ and $|+\rangle/\sqrt2$. The second has a nonzero $|1\rangle$ component while the first does not, so they are <linearly independent>. Therefore **the third state is also <entangled>**. <Orthogonality> of these two conditional <vectors> is not required for this conclusion.

For an independent <matrix> check, expanding the second factor gives
$$
C=\begin{pmatrix}1/\sqrt2&0\\1/2&1/2\end{pmatrix},\qquad \det C=\frac1{2\sqrt2}\ne0.
$$
The original expression is normalized because its two first-wire factors are <orthogonal>: its squared <norm> is $(1+1)/2=1$, despite the overlap between $|0\rangle$ and $|+\rangle$ on the second wire.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Measure the first <qutrit> in the <computational basis> and the second in the <orthonormal basis> $\{|\phi_0\rangle,|\phi_1\rangle,|\phi_2\rangle\}$ from Question 1(a), with those labels as outcomes. For first-party outcome 0, the unnormalized conditional <vector> of the second party is $|0\rangle/\sqrt3$, so this outcome occurs with <probability> $1/3$ and leaves the normalized state $|0\rangle$.

The second-party <Born rule> <probabilities> are then $|\langle\phi_0|0\rangle|^2=1/2$, $|\langle\phi_1|0\rangle|^2=0$, and $|\langle\phi_2|0\rangle|^2=1/2$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\Pr(B=0\mid A=0)=\Pr(B=2\mid A=0)=\tfrac12,\qquad\Pr(B=1\mid A=0)=0.}
$$
The relevant joint <probabilities> are $1/6,0,1/6$, each divided by $\Pr(A=0)=1/3$. This distinguishes the conditional statement from an unconditional outcome <probability>. More generally, <conjugate-state steering from a maximally entangled pair> explains how the first local measurement determines the second party's conditional <vector>; the <basis> choice here makes that <vector> especially simple.

=== d
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Fix the two subsystem dimensions. For <reflexivity>, $|\Psi\rangle=(I_d\otimes I_{d'})|\Psi\rangle$, and both <identity operators> are unitary.

For symmetry, if $|\Psi\rangle=(U\otimes V)|\Phi\rangle$, apply the inverse <local unitary transformation>:
$$
|\Phi\rangle=(U^\dagger\otimes V^\dagger)|\Psi\rangle.
$$
The <conjugate transposes> of <unitary operators> are unitary, so the reverse relation holds.

For <transitivity>, if additionally $|\Phi\rangle=(R\otimes S)|\Theta\rangle$, the <tensor product> multiplication rule gives
$$
|\Psi\rangle=(U\otimes V)(R\otimes S)|\Theta\rangle=(UR\otimes VS)|\Theta\rangle.
$$
Both products are unitary, for example $(UR)^\dagger(UR)=R^\dagger U^\dagger UR=I$. Thus **<local-unitary equivalence> is reflexive, symmetric and transitive**, and hence is an <equivalence relation>.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Use $S=\operatorname{diag}(1,i)=T^2$ and the <Pauli X gate>. Starting with the <Bell state> $|\Phi^+\rangle$, the exact constructions are
$$
\boxed{|\Psi_1\rangle=(S\otimes X)|\Phi^+\rangle,\qquad |\Psi_2\rangle=(I\otimes H)|\Phi^+\rangle.}
$$
For the first, $I\otimes X$ changes the two terms to $|01\rangle$ and $|10\rangle$, and $S\otimes I$ multiplies just the second term by $i$. For the second,
$$
(I\otimes H)|\Phi^+\rangle=\frac{|0\rangle|+\rangle+|1\rangle|-\rangle}{\sqrt2}=\frac{|00\rangle+|01\rangle+|10\rangle-|11\rangle}{2}.
$$
All factors are single-party <unitary operators>, proving both claimed <local-unitary equivalences>. The equations hold as <vectors>, without even an additional <global phase>.

==== iii
{scope}

===== Solution

Extend the normalized <vector> $|\phi\rangle$ to an <orthonormal basis> $\{|f_j\rangle\}_{j=1}^d$ with $|f_1\rangle=|\phi\rangle$, and extend $|\psi\rangle$ to an <orthonormal basis> $\{|e_j\rangle\}_{j=1}^d$ with $|e_1\rangle=|\psi\rangle$. The <Gram-Schmidt process> provides such extensions. Define
$$
U=\sum_{j=1}^d|e_j\rangle\langle f_j|.
$$
Then $U^\dagger U=\sum_j|f_j\rangle\langle f_j|=I$ and $UU^\dagger=I$, so $U$ is unitary and $U|\phi\rangle=|\psi\rangle$. Construct $V$ on the second subsystem in the same way, sending $|\phi'\rangle$ to $|\psi'\rangle$. Consequently
$$
(U\otimes V)(|\phi\rangle\otimes|\phi'\rangle)=|\psi\rangle\otimes|\psi'\rangle.
$$
Thus **any two normalized <product states> on the same pair of subsystem spaces are <local-unitary equivalent>**. The dimension and <normalization> requirements matter because a unitary preserves both its domain and <vector> <norms>.

==== iv
{scope}

===== Solution

A <local unitary transformation> preserves a product factorization:
$$
(U\otimes V)(|a\rangle\otimes|b\rangle)=(U|a\rangle)\otimes(V|b\rangle).
$$
The output is therefore another <product state>. If an <entangled state> could be locally mapped to a <product state>, applying the inverse $U^\dagger\otimes V^\dagger$ would make the original <entangled state> a product as well, a contradiction. Hence **a <product state> and an <entangled state> are never <local-unitary equivalent>**.

In terms of a <tensor-product coefficient matrix>, $C$ changes to $UCV^T$, with an ordinary <transpose> on the second factor. Both multiplying <matrices> are invertible, so the <matrix rank>, equivalently the <Schmidt rank>, is unchanged. This supplies an algebraic form of the same argument: a nonzero <pure state> is a <product state> exactly when that rank is one.

== 3
{scope}

Throughout this question let $|A_0\rangle=\sum_{f(x)=0}|x\rangle$, $|A_1\rangle=\sum_{f(x)=1}|x\rangle$, and $|J\rangle=\sum_{x=0}^{N-1}|x\rangle=\sqrt N\,|e_0\rangle$. These subset sums are unnormalized: $\langle A_j|A_j\rangle=S_j$ and $\langle A_0|A_1\rangle=0$. The promise implies that $N>0$ is divisible by four. No power-of-two assumption is needed for the abstract $N$-dimensional calculation.

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Substitute the promised cardinalities:
$$
\frac{2(S_0-S_1)}N=\frac2N\left(\frac{3N}4-\frac N4\right)=\frac2N\frac N2=\boxed{1}.
$$
The marked and unmarked subsets also exhaust the <computational basis>, so $S_0+S_1=N$; this will ensure that the uniform initial state has unit <norm>.

=== b
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

By orthonormality of the <computational basis>,
$$
\langle e_0|x\rangle=\frac1{\sqrt N}\sum_{y=0}^{N-1}\langle y|x\rangle=\frac1{\sqrt N}\sum_{y=0}^{N-1}\delta_{yx}=\boxed{\frac1{\sqrt N}}.
$$
There is one nonzero term, the term with $y=x$. In particular, the uniform coefficient is unchanged when passing from the ket to its bra because it is real.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Summing part (i) over the $S_0$ unmarked indices gives $\langle e_0|A_0\rangle=S_0/\sqrt N$. The <Grover diffusion operator> therefore acts as
$$
W|A_0\rangle=2|e_0\rangle\langle e_0|A_0\rangle-|A_0\rangle=\frac{2S_0}{\sqrt N}|e_0\rangle-|A_0\rangle=\boxed{\frac{2S_0}N|J\rangle-|A_0\rangle.}
$$
This is an identity for an unnormalized subset sum; it does not require replacing its <norm> by one.

==== iii
{scope}

===== Solution

For the marked subset, $\langle e_0|A_1\rangle=S_1/\sqrt N$ by the same summation of the individual <inner products>. Consequently
$$
W|A_1\rangle=2|e_0\rangle\frac{S_1}{\sqrt N}-|A_1\rangle=\boxed{\frac{2S_1}N|J\rangle-|A_1\rangle.}
$$
The cardinality is $S_1$ here. Its sign will reverse when this expression is used for the oracle's negative marked component.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

The <phase oracle> multiplies an unmarked <basis> <vector> by $+1$ and a marked one by $-1$. Thus the state after the oracle is
$$
\boxed{V_f|e_0\rangle=\frac1{\sqrt N}\bigl(|A_0\rangle-|A_1\rangle\bigr).}
$$
Its squared <norm> is $(S_0+S_1)/N=1$. All computational outcomes would still have <probability> $1/N$ if measured at this point: the oracle has changed the <relative phase>, not the magnitudes of the amplitudes. The following reflection converts that phase information into a changed <probability distribution> through <quantum interference>.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

Apply parts (b)(ii) and (b)(iii), keeping the oracle's minus sign:
$$
\begin{aligned}
WV_f|e_0\rangle
&=\frac1{\sqrt N}\left[\frac{2(S_0-S_1)}N|J\rangle-|A_0\rangle+|A_1\rangle\right]\\
&=\frac1{\sqrt N}\bigl[|J\rangle-|A_0\rangle+|A_1\rangle\bigr]\\
&=\boxed{\frac2{\sqrt N}|A_1\rangle=\frac2{\sqrt N}\sum_{f(x)=1}|x\rangle.}
\end{aligned}
$$
The middle equality uses part (a), and the last uses $|J\rangle=|A_0\rangle+|A_1\rangle$. The final squared <norm> is $4S_1/N=1$, providing a <normalization> check.

The sign convention matters: here $W=2|e_0\rangle\langle e_0|-I$ fixes $|e_0\rangle$ and negates its <orthogonal> complement. Its action on amplitudes is <inversion about the mean>. The negative of this reflection would change the final <vector> by an overall <global phase>, but mixing those conventions midway would give incorrect intermediate signs.<A fast quantum mechanical algorithm for database search by Lov Grover>[Section 4, diffusion transform and equation (4.0), inversion-about-average interpretation]{ref}

=== e
{scope}

==== Solution

The algorithm **returns a uniformly random marked input with certainty**. The <Born rule> applied to the final state gives, for each individual <basis> label $y$,
$$
\boxed{\Pr(Y=y)=\begin{cases}4/N=1/S_1,&f(y)=1,\\0,&f(y)=0.\end{cases}}
$$
Summing over the $S_1=N/4$ marked inputs gives $\Pr(f(Y)=1)=1$. The outcome is a marked input label, not merely the bit indicating whether some marked input exists. There is one use of the supplied <phase oracle>, together with the specified preparation, reflection and measurement.

The exact success relies on the marked fraction $r=S_1/N=1/4$. More generally, the mean amplitude immediately after the oracle is $(1-2r)/\sqrt N$. <Inversion about the mean> sends an unmarked amplitude to $(1-4r)/\sqrt N$ and a marked amplitude to $(3-4r)/\sqrt N$. Thus a single iteration has marked success <probability> $r(3-4r)^2$. At $r=1/4$ the unmarked amplitudes vanish and the marked amplitudes become $2/\sqrt N$, giving the <one-step exact Grover search with a quarter of the inputs marked>. No claim of one-query certainty for an arbitrary marked fraction is needed.

\Image[quantum-information-quarter-marked-search.png]
{title=Original amplitude and Born-probability illustration for eight inputs with two marked. The oracle's relative minus signs become exact concentration on the marked inputs after one reflection.}
